course: everyday
lesson: 46
title: Sun safety
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sunlight carries invisible ultraviolet rays, or UV; they cannot be felt as heat, but they burn the skin and over many years can cause skin cancer.
explain: UV is strongest in the middle of the day, in summer and in sunny places such as Australia and New Zealand, and it gets through thin clouds, so you can burn on a cool, cloudy day.
explain: In Australia children learn to slip on a shirt, slop on sunscreen, slap on a hat, seek shade and slide on sunglasses.
explain: Sunscreen goes on before you go out and is put on again every two hours and after swimming, because it rubs and washes off.
explain: In hot weather you also drink water often; a girl in Arizona who plays all afternoon in the heat without drinking may get a headache and feel dizzy and sick, which is a sign to go to the shade, cool down, drink and tell an adult.
explain: A girl in Queensland spent a whole day at the beach without sun cream; she got burnt so badly she had blisters and a fever and could not sleep.
explain: A toddler in Arizona was left asleep in a parked car for a short time; the car heated up fast and he had to be rushed to hospital with heatstroke.
fact: The Sun gives out invisible ultraviolet, or UV, rays that burn the skin.
fact: You cannot feel UV rays, so you can burn even when it does not feel hot.
fact: Much of the UV gets through clouds, so you can burn on a cloudy day.
fact: UV is strongest around the middle of the day.
fact: Australia and New Zealand have some of the highest rates of skin cancer in the world.
fact: Sunscreen should be at least SPF 30 (SPF is the number that shows how strongly it protects) and be put on again every two hours and after swimming.
fact: A hat with a wide brim shades your face, ears and neck.
fact: Water, sand and snow reflect UV, so you can burn even under an umbrella or on a ski slope.
fact: Sunburn on a child's skin raises the chance of skin cancer later in life.
fact: People with darker skin can still get sunburnt and still need sun protection.
fact: In hot weather you need to drink water often, before you feel very thirsty.
fact: A parked car can become dangerously hot in minutes, so children and pets must never be left inside one.
fact: A tan is not a sign of healthy skin; it is a sign that UV has damaged the skin.
exercise: What part of sunlight burns your skin? | Invisible ultraviolet rays | The bright yellow light you see | The heat you feel on your skin | The light reflected by the Moon
exercise: Why can you get sunburnt on a cool, windy day? | UV rays burn you even when you do not feel hot | Wind blows the sunburn onto your skin from far away | Cold air makes your skin weaker | You cannot, sunburn needs a hot day
exercise: Can you get sunburnt on a cloudy day? | Yes, much of the UV gets through the clouds | No, clouds stop all the UV from reaching the ground | Only if it is also raining | Only in the early morning
exercise: When is the Sun's UV strongest? | Around the middle of the day | Early in the morning, before breakfast | Late in the evening, after tea | At sunset on a cold winter day
exercise: What do Australian children mean by slip, slop, slap? | Slip on a shirt, slop on sunscreen, slap on a hat | Slip into the pool, slop water, slap the waves | Slip off shoes, slop on mud, slap on a cap | Slip on sandals, slop on cool lotion, slap on goggles
exercise: How often should you put sunscreen on again while playing outside? | About every two hours and after swimming | Once in the morning is enough for the day | Only when your skin turns red | Once a week in summer
exercise: Which hat protects you best from the sun? | One with a wide brim all round | A baseball cap worn backwards on your head | A thin headband | A woolly hat with no brim
exercise: Why can you burn while sitting under a beach umbrella? | Sand and water reflect UV up at you | The umbrella makes the light stronger | Umbrellas let all the heat straight through | You cannot ever burn in the shade at all
exercise: Why do skiers in Colorado need sunscreen on a sunny winter day? | Snow reflects UV back onto the skin | Cold air makes the sun hotter | The snow is warm high up in the mountains | They do not need it in winter
exercise: Why do doctors want children to avoid sunburn? | Sunburn in childhood raises the chance of skin cancer later | Sunburn only hurts for a day or two and does no harm at all after that | Sunburn makes you grow more slowly | Sunburn is only bad for adults
exercise: Is it true that people with dark skin never get sunburnt? | No, they can still burn and still need protection | Yes, dark skin blocks all UV | Yes, but only in Australia | No, but only on cloudy days in the middle of summer
exercise: When should you drink water on a hot day? | Often, before you feel very thirsty | Only when your mouth is completely dry | Only at lunchtime, with your sandwiches | Only after you have finished playing
exercise: Why must a child never be left in a parked car on a warm day? | The car can heat up to dangerous levels in minutes | The car might be stolen | The child might press the horn | Cars use up all the air inside them in a few seconds
exercise: Which is the best place to play at noon on a very sunny day? | In the shade, wearing a hat | In the open, without a hat | On bright white sand | On a sunny rooftop
test: A boy in Brisbane says he does not need a hat because the day is cloudy and cool. What would you tell him? | UV gets through clouds and burns without feeling hot | He is right, clouds block all the sunburn on a cool day | He only needs a hat at sunset | He only needs a hat if he is swimming
test: A girl in Perth put on sunscreen at nine in the morning and has been swimming all morning. What should she do at noon? | Put sunscreen on again and find some shade | Nothing, the morning sunscreen still works fine | Swim some more, since the water stops all UV | Wait until she turns red, then put some more on
test: A family in Texas plans a long bike ride in July. Which time of day is best for the ride? | Early morning, before the strongest sun | Around noon, when it is brightest and warmest | Early afternoon, after lunch | It makes no difference
test: Which is true? | Sunburn happens even when you do not feel hot | You only burn when you feel the heat on your skin | UV rays are the warm part of sunlight you feel | Clouds always stop all of the Sun's UV from getting through
test: A girl in Arizona has played football for hours in the heat. She feels dizzy, has a headache and feels sick. What should she do? | Go into the shade, drink water, cool down and tell an adult | Keep playing so she does not let her team down before the end of the match | Lie in the sun to rest | Drink nothing so she does not feel worse
test: A dad needs to buy one thing quickly in a shop on a hot day. What should he do with his little son? | Take him into the shop with him | Leave him in the car with the windows closed | Leave him in the car for just five minutes | Leave him in the car with the engine off
test: A girl from Christchurch is going skiing on a bright day. What should she take for her face? | Sunscreen and sunglasses or goggles | Nothing, as the snow keeps her face cool | Only a scarf over her mouth | Only lip balm, nothing else
test: Why do Australian schools often have a rule of no hat, no play? | Australia has very strong UV and lots of skin cancer | Hats help children run faster | Hats keep the playground cleaner | Hats keep children warm on cold winter mornings in the playground
test: A boy says he is fine because he put on sunscreen once before school. He plays outside all day. What is wrong? | Sunscreen wears off and must be put on again | Nothing, once is enough all day | Sunscreen only works at school | Sunscreen slowly gets stronger as the day goes on
test: cloudy today, no sunscreen yeah? | You still need it, UV gets through clouds | No, you can't burn when it's cloudy, ever | Only if it feels hot | Only after 5pm
test: put sunscreen on at 9, still good at 3? | Put it on again every 2 hours | Yes, it lasts all day | Yes, if you don't swim | Yes, if it was SPF 50
test: my skin's dark, don't need sunscreen | You can still burn, you need protection | Dark skin never burns, not even in summer | Only pale skin needs it | Only on very hot days
test: just popping in the shop, leave the dog in the car? | No, cars get dangerously hot in minutes | Yes, for five minutes | Yes, if a window is open a crack, dogs are fine | Yes, if it's shady
test: it's cloudy so no need for sun cream right | You still need it, the sun burns through clouds | Right, clouds block all of it | Only if it is hot | Only on your face
test: baby's asleep in the car, let's just pop into the shop | Never leave a child in a car, it gets hot fast | It is fine for five minutes | It is fine with a window open and the car parked in the shade | It is fine in the shade
test: Ella says a tan means ur skin is healthy. right? | No, a tan is a sign of skin damage | Yes, tanned skin is stronger | Yes, it means you got loads of vitamins | Yes, unless you peel
test: heard Mia got so burnt on a cloudy day at the beach her skin peeled off in one big sheet lol | Real lesson: UV gets through clouds, so wear sunscreen; the sheet bit is likely exaggerated | Real lesson: cloudy days are the only days you burn | It proves the beach is always dangerous | It is gossip, so clouds protect you fully and nobody ever burns on a cloudy day at the beach
test: my brother says u can't get sunburnt skiing cos it's cold | Wrong; snow reflects UV, so you can burn skiing even in the cold | He is right, cold stops UV | He is right, if you wear gloves | He is right, but only in the morning before the sun is high up over the slopes
test: Liam's got a big red sunburn and people are calling him Lobster, should i say it too | No, nicknames about his skin are unkind, and sunburn hurts | Yes, because it is only a joke | Yes, so he remembers to wear sunscreen next time he goes outside | Yes, but only when he cannot hear
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises ultraviolet radiation from the Sun? | It is invisible, cannot be felt, and burns the skin | It is visible as bright yellow light | It is felt as heat upon the skin | It is entirely blocked by clouds of every kind
exam: Identify the time of day at which ultraviolet radiation is strongest. | Around the middle of the day | Early in the morning | Late in the evening | Shortly after sunset
exam: It being given that sunscreen is employed, select the practice consistent with established guidance. | A factor of at least thirty, reapplied every two hours and after swimming | A factor of five, applied once in the morning only | Any factor, applied after the skin has burned | A factor of thirty, applied once a week, which suffices for all outdoor activity
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning sunburn in childhood. | It increases the likelihood of skin cancer later in life | It has no consequence for later health | It protects the skin against all future burns | It affects only children in cold countries
